+and then a thirteenth bar of Cm7 to finish on. Bars 1 to 8, 11 and 12 follow
+the major blues of *Walking bass over a 12-bar blues in C* chord for chord,
+with the tonic and the IV as minor sevenths, i7 and iv7: Cm7 is C, E♭, G and
+B♭, and Fm7 is F, A♭, C and E♭. G7, the V7, is a dominant seventh in both,
+with B natural, a semitone below C. Bars 9 and 10 are where the two differ.
+
+**Bars 9 and 10 have more than one version.** This walking-bass exercise uses
+♭VI7 to V7 in bars 9 and 10: A♭7, then G7. Minor-blues changes vary; the
+Lab's minor blues uses V7 to iv7 there instead: G7, then Fm7. Both are in use,
+and neither one is the minor blues; when this page means one, it names it. In
+D minor, ♭VI7 to V7 is B♭7 to A7, which you will meet again in *St. James
+Infirmary*.
+
+**The walking line in minor.** The left hand plays the same practice pattern,
+root, third, fifth and a semitone below the next root, so it takes each
+chord's own third: E♭ over Cm7 and A♭ over Fm7, but C over A♭7 and B over G7.
+The last bar is root, third, fifth and the root an octave up. The right hand
+holds one chord a bar: its root, third and seventh, Cm7 as C, E♭ and B♭.
+
+**Step by step, in C minor.**
+
+1. Open *Walking bass over a 12-bar blues in C* and press *Hear it*; then *Walking bass over a minor 12-bar blues in C minor*, and *Hear it* again. Listen for the minor chords, and for bars 9 and 10.
+2. In the C minor exercise choose *Keep tempo* and press **L** on the control bar: you play the left hand, and the app plays the right hand's chords while *Duet*, in the ⋯ controls, is on, as it is unless you have turned it off. This run does not count (below); it is for the left hand alone.
+3. Then **Both**, in *Keep tempo*: the walking line under the written chords. This is the run this page counts.
+4. The same with *Walking bass over a minor 12-bar blues in F minor*, both hands, at a lower tempo first.
+5. Tap *Chart* beside the C minor exercise. Turn *Bass + drums* on, leave *Comp* off, press *Count off ▶* and play the right hand's chords as the exercise writes them, root, third and seventh, over the app's bass and drums, your left hand resting. The cell of the bar that is sounding marks what you hold against that bar's chord: the exercise's three notes read as a match; the third and the seventh alone, two notes of four, do not.
+
+**St. James Infirmary: a minor tune to walk and comp.** *St. James
+Infirmary* is in D minor, and it is not a twelve-bar blues. Its 24 bars are a
+16-bar verse, bars 1 to 16, and an 8-bar refrain, bars 17 to 24. Its chords are
+Dm, Gm, B♭7 and A7, with one D7 in bar 5 and one F7 in bar 22. Bars 7 and 8 are
+B♭7 then A7, the ♭VI7 to V7 of D minor. Many bars hold two or three chords:
+bar 1 is Dm for two beats, then B♭7 for one and A7 for one. On the chart each
+chord stands where it starts in its bar.
+
+1. Open *St. James Infirmary* from this page and press *Hear it*.
+2. Tap *Chart* beside it, turn *Comp* and *Bass + drums* on, press *Count off ▶* and follow the 24 bars while the app plays the chords.
+3. Turn *Bass + drums* off and keep *Comp* on: walk a bass line in your left hand under the app's chords, one note to a beat, each chord's root on the beat it starts. A beat left before a change can lead into the next root by a semitone, as in the exercises. Ignore the cell in this step: it checks for a chord, and one bass note is never enough for it to say yes.
+4. Then *Comp* off and *Bass + drums* on: the chords in your right hand over the app's bass and drums, the left hand resting. Here the cell is worth watching.
+5. Last, both off: the walking line in the left hand and the chords in the right, through the 24 bars.
+
+**The quick IV.** Tap *Chart* beside *St. Louis Blues*. Its first twelve bars
+are a twelve-bar blues in E, and bar 2 is A7, the IV7: where the twelve-bar
+you have played so far stays on the I for four bars, this one goes to the IV in
+bar 2 and back to E in bar 3. An IV that early is called the quick IV, or the
+quick change, because it comes in bar 2 rather than bar 5. With *Comp* and
+*Bass + drums* off, press *Count off ▶*, play the roots in the left hand and
+the chords in the right through bars 1 to 12, then press *Stop*. When you come
+to play choruses of your own, on a later blues rung, the quick IV in bar 2 is a
+choice you can make; nothing in the app checks it.
+
+**On your own.** Tap *Chart* beside *Walking bass over a minor 12-bar blues in
+B♭ minor*, a key you have not practised. Leave *Comp* and *Bass + drums* off,
+and do not press *Count off ▶*: no click, no count-in and no bar moving along.
+Count yourself in, and play the twelve bars and the last one with the walking
+line in your left hand and the chords in your right, reading only the chord
+symbols. Without *Count off ▶* the chart does not move with you: it stays on
+bar 1 and checks whatever you hold against bar 1's chord, so its cell says
+nothing about this task. Ignore it. The app gives no verdict here; whether the
+walk, the chords and the form were right is yours to check.
+
+**What the app counts here.** Two exercise runs, each opened from this page.
+One passing run of any of the five exercises above the minor ones: the two
+boogies in C, the walking eighths in F, the walking bass over the major blues
+or the turnaround. And one passing run of *Walking bass over a minor 12-bar
+blues in C minor* with both hands: a run of the left hand with the app playing
+the right, or of either hand alone, does not count. A passing run is 90 %
+right, played in *Keep tempo*, from the first bar to the last, at 85 % of its
+written tempo or more. The F minor and B♭ minor walking exercises, *St. James
+Infirmary* and *St. Louis Blues* count toward neither. The app measures the run
+in C minor. The chart work, walking and comping *St. James Infirmary*, the
+quick IV and the B♭ minor task are yours to check: they do not become checked
+by the app because the rung is met.
 
